🎯 Objective

Demonstrate offensive security fundamentals by discovering hidden functionality on a web application and exploiting it to modify account balances.

🛠 Step 1: Enumeration
Web Directory Enumeration

To identify hidden resources on the target web application (FakeBank), I used Gobuster to brute-force directory and page names:

gobuster -u http://fakebank.thm -w wordlist.txt dir

Result:
Found several endpoints including a hidden admin bank transfer page (/bank-transfer).

🔍 Step 2: Vulnerability Identification

The /bank-transfer page was not protected behind any authentication, allowing access to functionality intended only for privileged users. This type of exposed admin endpoint can lead to unauthorized actions.

💥 Step 3: Exploitation

I navigated to the discovered hidden bank transfer page in the FakeBank application using a web browser. After accessing this page, I executed an unauthorized transfer of funds from another account into my own.

🏁 Step 4: Outcome

The transfer was successful, and the new account balance reflected the added funds — demonstrating how insecure endpoints can be abused when not properly secured.

📖 Lessons Learned

Web directory brute-forcing is a powerful enumeration technique for finding hidden pages on a target domain.

Exposed admin pages can provide direct access to critical functionality if not secured correctly.

Gobuster is an effective tool for web enumeration commonly used in offensive security assessments.

🧰 Tools Used

Gobuster — for discovering hidden directories and pages.
